    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet2/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, interfaces are enabled by default (no \"shutdown\" equivalent needed), so to achieve the same effect as Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\", you would remove any disable statement. The above translation shows how to ensure the interfaces are not disabled. \\n\\nAlternatively, if you specifically need to enable an interface that was previously disabled, you would use:\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 disable\\n##\\nBut this would be the opposite of \"no shutdown\". The first translation is more accurate since Juniper interfaces are enabled by default.', 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet2/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet2/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, interfaces are enabled by default (no \"shutdown\" equivalent needed), so to achieve the same effect as Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\", you would remove any disable statement. The above translation shows how to ensure the interfaces are not disabled. \\n\\nAlternatively, if you specifically need to enable an interface that was previously disabled, you would use:\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 disable\\n##\\nBut this would be the opposite of \"no shutdown\". The first translation is more accurate since Juniper interfaces are enabled by default.', 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet2/0/0 disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet2/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, interfaces are enabled by default (no \"shutdown\" equivalent needed), so to achieve the same effect as Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\", you would remove any disable statement. The above translation shows how to ensure the interfaces are not disabled. \\n\\nAlternatively, if you specifically need to enable an interface that was previously disabled, you would use:\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 disable\\n##\\nBut this would be the opposite of \"no shutdown\". The first translation is more accurate since Juniper interfaces are enabled by default.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet2/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, interfaces are enabled by default (no \"shutdown\" equivalent needed), so to achieve the same effect as Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\", you would remove any disable statement. The above translation shows how to ensure the interfaces are not disabled. \\n\\nAlternatively, if you specifically need to enable an interface that was previously disabled, you would use:\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 disable\\n##\\nBut this would be the opposite of \"no shutdown\". The first translation is more accurate since Juniper interfaces are enabled by default.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 disable'}]]": 1,
