    "['set mpls-label', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set policy-options policy-statement policy1 term 10 then next-hop self', 'parent_command': ['set protocols ospf import [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set protocols ospf import [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent command for `set mpls-label`:\\n\\n##\\nset policy-options policy-statement policy1 term 10 then next-hop self\\nset policy-options policy-statement policy1 term 10 then accept\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, there isn't a direct 1:1 equivalent to Cisco's `set mpls-label` in route-maps. The closest interpretation is that this Cisco command is used to push MPLS labels when advertising routes to BGP peers. In Juniper, this is typically handled implicitly when MPLS is configured on the interface and BGP is set up with `next-hop self`. The above commands maintain the same logical behavior as the original Cisco configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'set policy-options policy-statement policy1 term 10 then next-hop self'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set policy-options policy-statement policy1 term 10 then accept', 'parent_command': ['set protocols ospf import [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set protocols ospf import [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent command for `set mpls-label`:\\n\\n##\\nset policy-options policy-statement policy1 term 10 then next-hop self\\nset policy-options policy-statement policy1 term 10 then accept\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, there isn't a direct 1:1 equivalent to Cisco's `set mpls-label` in route-maps. The closest interpretation is that this Cisco command is used to push MPLS labels when advertising routes to BGP peers. In Juniper, this is typically handled implicitly when MPLS is configured on the interface and BGP is set up with `next-hop self`. The above commands maintain the same logical behavior as the original Cisco configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'set policy-options policy-statement policy1 term 10 then accept'}]]": 1,
